      "updated": 1781413075664,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Potential Deep Dives \n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Potential Deep Dives \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "o8p1LH90FA07OsazVtcYV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 943.774812706612,
      "y": -352.0010795139083,
      "width": 745.1612577027756,
      "height": 3150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#da77f2",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBZ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1041999971,
      "version": 3943,
      "versionNonce": 55999955,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781413075664,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Improved data model \n    - Users book a Room Type, not a specific room (assigned at check-in).\nAPIs now require roomTypeID. A new room_type_inventory table tracks\ndaily availability (hotel_id, room_type_id, date) and is pre-populated 2\nyears in advance.\n    - Standard Check: Queries the database to ensure (total_reserved +\nrequestedRooms) <= total_inventory for every requested date.\n10% Overbooking Check: Simply modifies the logic to <= (total_inventory *\n1.1).\n    - Future Scaling: Archive historical reservations to cold storage and\nhorizontally shard active data using hotel_id as the key.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2) Concurrency issues \n    A) One User Clicks \"Book\" Multiple Times\n    - The Problem: An impatient user double-clicks the \"Complete Booking\"\nbutton, potentially charging their card twice and booking two identical\nreservations.\n    - The Solutions:\n        > Client-Side UI Fix: Disable or hide the \"Submit\" button after the\nfirst click. This is a good basic step, but it is unreliable because a savvy\nuser can bypass it by disabling JavaScript in their browser.\n        > Idempotency Key (The Real Fix): Create a globally unique ID\n(reservation_id) for the booking before the user clicks submit. When they\nclick the button, this ID goes to the database. Because the database\nenforces this ID as a unique primary key, any extra clicks carrying that\nexact same ID are simply ignored.\n\n    B) Multiple Users Try to Book the Last Room (Race Condition)\n    - The Problem: There is 1 room left. User A and User B try to book it\nat the exact same time. The system checks the inventory for User A (1\nroom left) and simultaneously checks for User B (1 room left). Both are\napproved, both book the room, and the hotel is overbooked.\n    - The Solutions:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3) Scaling the system\n    - Database Sharding\n        > The Strategy: Split the massive central database into multiple\nsmaller databases (shards), dividing the total traffic load into manageable\npieces.\n        > How to Split: Since almost all queries search by a specific hotel,\ndivide the data based on hotel_id. (e.g., Shard A handles Hotels 1-1000,\nShard B handles Hotels 1001-2000).\n\n    - Caching (Using Redis)\n        > The Strategy: Put a high-speed memory layer (a cache) in front of\nthe database to handle the massive volume of \"Are there rooms available?\"\nsearches. Only send actual booking requests (writes) to the main database.\n        > The Inconsistency Trade-off: Because the database and cache\nupdate at slightly different speeds, a user might see a room available in the\ncache that was just booked in the database. This is acceptable because\nthe database serves as the ultimate \"source of truth\" and will catch the\nerror before confirming the booking.\n\n4) Resolving data inconsistency in the microservice architecture\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    - The Architecture Dilemma\n        > The Hybrid Approach (Recommended here): By keeping the\nReservation and Inventory data in the same relational database, the\nsystem can use built-in, reliable database safety nets (ACID properties) to\nensure that if a booking fails, everything rolls back automatically.\n        > The \"Pure\" Microservice Problem: If you strictly separate every\nservice into its own individual database, a single booking requires updating\nmultiple databases at once. If the inventory database updates but the\nreservation database suddenly crashes, your data is out of sync.\n\n    - Solution A: Two-Phase Commit (2PC)\n        > How it works: A strict \"all-or-nothing\" protocol that forces all the\nseparate databases to lock hands and commit to the data change at the\nexact same millisecond.\n        > The downside: It acts like a traffic jam. Because it forces all\ndatabases to wait for each other, a single slow or crashed server will\nfreeze the entire transaction process, making it terrible for system\nperformance.\n\n    - Solution B: The Saga Pattern\n        > How it works: A relay race of smaller, local transactions. If step 3\nof a booking fails, the system fires off a sequence of automated \"undo\"\nmessages (compensating transactions) to reverse the work done in steps 1\nand 2.\n        > The downside: It is incredibly complex to code and test.\nFurthermore, it relies on \"eventual consistency,\" meaning there is a brief\nwindow where different parts of your system will show different, conflicting\ndata while the undo messages are still traveling.\n\n5) Latency : same things we can use elastic search for this. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Improved data model \n    - Users book a Room Type, not a specific room (assigned at check-in). APIs now require roomTypeID. A new room_type_inventory table tracks daily availability (hotel_id, room_type_id, date) and is pre-populated 2 years in advance.\n    - Standard Check: Queries the database to ensure (total_reserved + requestedRooms) <= total_inventory for every requested date.\n10% Overbooking Check: Simply modifies the logic to <= (total_inventory * 1.1).\n    - Future Scaling: Archive historical reservations to cold storage and horizontally shard active data using hotel_id as the key.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2) Concurrency issues \n    A) One User Clicks \"Book\" Multiple Times\n    - The Problem: An impatient user double-clicks the \"Complete Booking\" button, potentially charging their card twice and booking two identical reservations.\n    - The Solutions:\n        > Client-Side UI Fix: Disable or hide the \"Submit\" button after the first click. This is a good basic step, but it is unreliable because a savvy user can bypass it by disabling JavaScript in their browser.\n        > Idempotency Key (The Real Fix): Create a globally unique ID (reservation_id) for the booking before the user clicks submit. When they click the button, this ID goes to the database. Because the database enforces this ID as a unique primary key, any extra clicks carrying that exact same ID are simply ignored.\n\n    B) Multiple Users Try to Book the Last Room (Race Condition)\n    - The Problem: There is 1 room left. User A and User B try to book it at the exact same time. The system checks the inventory for User A (1 room left) and simultaneously checks for User B (1 room left). Both are approved, both book the room, and the hotel is overbooked.\n    - The Solutions:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3) Scaling the system\n    - Database Sharding\n        > The Strategy: Split the massive central database into multiple smaller databases (shards), dividing the total traffic load into manageable pieces.\n        > How to Split: Since almost all queries search by a specific hotel, divide the data based on hotel_id. (e.g., Shard A handles Hotels 1-1000, Shard B handles Hotels 1001-2000).\n\n    - Caching (Using Redis)\n        > The Strategy: Put a high-speed memory layer (a cache) in front of the database to handle the massive volume of \"Are there rooms available?\" searches. Only send actual booking requests (writes) to the main database.\n        > The Inconsistency Trade-off: Because the database and cache update at slightly different speeds, a user might see a room available in the cache that was just booked in the database. This is acceptable because the database serves as the ultimate \"source of truth\" and will catch the error before confirming the booking.\n\n4) Resolving data inconsistency in the microservice architecture\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    - The Architecture Dilemma\n        > The Hybrid Approach (Recommended here): By keeping the Reservation and Inventory data in the same relational database, the system can use built-in, reliable database safety nets (ACID properties) to ensure that if a booking fails, everything rolls back automatically.\n        > The \"Pure\" Microservice Problem: If you strictly separate every service into its own individual database, a single booking requires updating multiple databases at once. If the inventory database updates but the reservation database suddenly crashes, your data is out of sync.\n\n    - Solution A: Two-Phase Commit (2PC)\n        > How it works: A strict \"all-or-nothing\" protocol that forces all the separate databases to lock hands and commit to the data change at the exact same millisecond.\n        > The downside: It acts like a traffic jam. Because it forces all databases to wait for each other, a single slow or crashed server will freeze the entire transaction process, making it terrible for system performance.\n\n    - Solution B: The Saga Pattern\n        > How it works: A relay race of smaller, local transactions. If step 3 of a booking fails, the system fires off a sequence of automated \"undo\" messages (compensating transactions) to reverse the work done in steps 1 and 2.\n        > The downside: It is incredibly complex to code and test. Furthermore, it relies on \"eventual consistency,\" meaning there is a brief window where different parts of your system will show different, conflicting data while the undo messages are still traveling.\n\n5) Latency : same things we can use elastic search for this. ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
